    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\n```\ncdef IntOrFloat f(IntOrFloat x) except -1:\n                                        ^\n------------------------------------------------------------\nfusedreturn.pyx:5:40: Cannot coerce to a type that is not specialized\n```\n\nThis should work because `-1` would be a valid exception value for both `int` or `float`\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\ncdef fused IntOrFloat:\n    int\n    float\n\ncdef IntOrFloat f(IntOrFloat x) except -1:\n    return x\n```\n\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n_No response_\n\n### OS\n\nLinux\n\n### Python version\n\n3.13\n\n### Cython version\n\nCurrent master\n\n### Additional context\n\nPossibly worth checking if https://github.com/cython/cython/pull/5737 solves it/helps... It certainly contains a test that looks similar",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I looked into it and found that on encountering a function declaration with a fused type (a generic or template type), it tries to validate the function's error-handling return value (e.g., except -1) too early.\nIn Nodes.py, the compiler attempts to check if the error value can be converted (coerce_to) to the function's return type.\nAt this point in the compilation process, the return type is still just the placeholder \"fused type\" (e.g., a generic numeric type) rather than a specific, concrete type (like int or float) and because `ExprNodes.coerce_to` doesn't know how to convert a concrete number into an unspecialized, abstract fused type, it gives up and throws the error: \"Cannot coerce to a type that is not specialized.\"\n\nThis matches what you also quoted on #5737; the exception-value check needs to be deferred until FusedNode specializes the return type and is validated against each specialization individually, rather than checked against the bare fused type at declaration time.",
